nce Pipelines and for training runs (passive-component validation: Data Science Pipelines requires no code changes and its run data feeds the comparison unchanged).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 (P0): select two or more runs, view parameters and metrics side by side in the Dashboard.\n- HLR-3 (P1): diff view highlighting changed parameters and metrics between two selected runs — bundled here because it is a presentation mode of the same comparison surface over the same run data as HLR-1; a separate epic would build the same surface twice.\n\nE002 body:\n\n# Run lineage on registered model versions\n\n## Description\nRegistered model versions record a reference to the run (training or pipeline) that produced them, exposed through the Model Registry's interfaces so a run comparison can be opened from the registry.\n\n## Scope\n- Run reference on registered model versions: a model version carries the identity of the run that produced it, using the model-version metadata extension point (custom properties).\n- The run reference is retrievable via the Model Registry REST API so registry clients (including the Dashboard's registry view) can resolve the producing run.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A registered model version can carry a reference to the run that produced it.\n- The run reference is retrievable through the Model Registry REST API.\n- The linkage mechanism uses the existing model-version metadata extension point without changing the REST API contract's existing resources. Hmm — \"without changing\" is a design constraint I'm inventing. Better: \"The run reference round-trips through the Model Registry REST API: write on registration/registration update, read by a client.\" Keep simpler:\n- AC: The run reference survives retrieval via the REST API (write then read returns the same run reference).\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-2 (P1): registered model versions link back to the run that produced them.\n\nE003 body:\n\n# Open a run comparison from the Model Registry\n\n## Description\nFrom the Model Registry view in the Dashboard, a user can open a run comparison seeded with the run linked to a registered model version, completing the registry-side entry point of the run lineage capability.\n\n## Scope\n- In the Dashboard's Model Registry view, surface the run linked to a registered model version (once model versions carry run references).\n- Action to open a run comparison seeded with the linked run, letting the user add further runs to compare against.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given a registered model version that carries a run reference, the registry view in the Dashboard offers an action that opens a run comparison containing that run.\n- The opened comparison renders the linked run's parameters and metrics and allows additional runs to be added to the comparison.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-2 (P1): a comparison can be opened from the Model Registry.\n\nE004 body (docs-authoring):\n\n# Documentation for the run comparison workflow\n\n## Description\nUser documentation covering the run comparison workflow from both entry points: the runs list in the Dashboard and the Model Registry.\n\n## Scope (content outline inside Scope)\n- Comparison from the runs list: selecting two or more runs, reading the side-by-side parameters and metrics, interpreting the diff highlighting between two runs.\n- Comparison from the Model Registry: opening a comparison from a registered model version linked to its producing run.\n- Where each entry point is available and how run lineage is recorded on registered model versions.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Documentation topics cover the comparison workflow starting from the runs list, including the two-run diff view.\n- Documentation topics cover the comparison workflow starting from the Model Registry via the model version's run lineage.\n- The documentation is technically reviewed against the implemented comparison capability and registry linkage before publication.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-4 (P2): documentation covers the comparison workflow from both the runs list and the registry.\n\nSignal rationale files — markdown tables with Signal, Value, Rationale.\n\nNow, the decomposition summary body:\n\n# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9015: Experiment comparison for training and pipeline runs\n\n## Epic List\n| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n| RHAISTRAT-9015-E001 | Run comparison view for training and pipeline runs in the Dashboard | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9015-E002 | Run lineage on registered model versions | Implementation | Model Registry | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9015-E003 | Open a run comparison from the Model Registry | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n| RHAISTRAT-9015-E004 | Documentation for the run comparison workflow | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n\nWait — E004 priority: docs-authoring exception, strategy frontmatter priority=Major → P1. Note that in summary.\n\n## Dependency DAG\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    E001 --> E003\n    E002 --> E003\n    E001 --> E004\n    E002 --> E004\n    E003 --> E004\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 12 (framework → consumer) | registry entry point opens the comparison surface; builds against it |\n| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (artifact-producing → consuming) | entry point consumes the run reference on model versions |\n| E001 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs last) | docs describe the built comparison |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 | docs describe registry lineage |\n| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 | docs describe the registry entry point |\n| (no edge) E001 ∥ E002 | Rule 14 | different repos, no shared artifacts |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 |\n| HLR-2 | P1 | E002, E003 |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | E001 (bundled) — diff view is a presentation mode of the same comparison surface over the same run data as HLR-1; strategy notes wording ove